KEYWORDS: DFT, Potential Energy Surface, 2D-cMOF, mixed ligand 2D-cMOF, chemiresistive sensor
Metal−organic frameworks (MOFs) are a class of porous materials consisting of metal-containing units with crystalline frameworks. The MOFs have been extensively studied over three decades for their superior structural tunability. Various combinations of metal nodes and organic linkers broaden their design strategies by allowing postsynthetic modification. Among the different types of MOFs, multicomponent MOFs that contain more than one type of organic ligand or metal ion have gained increasing attention due to their potential for exhibiting unique properties that are not present in single-component MOFs. This hybrid architecture has been adopted in many applications such as catalysts, drug delivery, and gas separation owing to the synergy effect between the multiple ligands (or metal ions) and
the original system. The multicomponent MOFs can be categorized into three-dimensional (3D) and two-dimensional (2D) structures, depending on their dimensionality. For 3DMOFs, Kim et al. reported the synergistic glucose-sensing performance of the mixed metallic 3D-MOF, ZIF- \(\cdot\mathrm{Zn}_{0.5}\mathrm{Co}_{0.5},\) compared to unmixed states, ZIF-8 and ZIF-67. Their
experimental result suggested that the electrocatalytic sensitivity of ZIF-67 combined with the chemical stability of ZIF-8 resulted in superior glucose sensitivity in ZIF- \(\cdot\mathrm{Zn}_{0.5}\mathrm{Co}_{0.5}\). In addition, Grau-Crespo et al. demonstrated a synergistic reduction in the bandgap of mixed ligand ZIFs for photocatalytic applications using computational modeling. A comparative analysis in the HOMO−LUMO energy gap of various organic ligands allowed the prediction of \(\mathrm{Co}(\mathrm{mlm})\) - (nlm) as a promising photocatalyst material based on a muchreduced bandgap compared to its unmixed states.
Unlike fully connected 3D-MOFs, 2D conductive MOFs (2D-cMOFs) have a layered 2D network allowing \(\pi{-}\mathrm{d}\) conjugation to form the efficient electron charge transfer pathway, promoting charge mobility and conductivity.2,11−16 Their superior electrical conductivity and high external surface area enable further extending the applications into electrochemical fields.12,17 Dong et al. reported that the 2D-cMOF with mixed functional groups, cobalt-2,3,6,7,10,11-hexathioltriphenylene-2,3,6,7,10,11-hexaiminotriphenylene (Co-HTTPHITP), exhibited superior electrocatalytic performance compared to the single-component 2D-cMOFs, Co-HTTP and Co-HITP.18 The experimental and DFT calculations revealed that Co-metal is the crucial active site in the HER process, and its hybrid coordination with thiol (SH) and amine \(\left(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\right)\) functional groups has a synergistic effect that facilitates the ad/desorption of \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\) compared to unmixed 2D-cMOFs.18 Despite the potential benefits of multicomponent MOFs, obtaining synergetic properties for gas-sensing applications is challenging. This difficulty arises from the lack of understanding of a gas−MOF interaction mechanism as the change in the chemical environment of gas adsorption sites. Campbell et al. first reported the chemiresistive gas-sensing application of 2D-cMOFs by showing the effective \(\mathrm{NH}_{3}\) gas-sensing performance of copper-2,3,6,7,10,11-hexaiminotriphenylene (CuHITP).19 From his discovery, the 2D-cMOFs have been widely studied as room temperature (RT) chemiresistive sensing materials for \(\mathrm{NH}_{3}\) .20,21 \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{S},^{22,23}\mathrm{~NO}_{x},^{22,24,25}\mathrm{~CO}_{2},^{26}\) and VOCs.11 However, most 2D-cMOF-based sensors focus on single metal and single organic ligand structures, limiting
the tunability of the electronic energy level and topology.27,28 Yao et al. first reported the successful synthesis of the mixed ligand 2D-cMOF, copper-tetrahydroxy-1,4-quinone-hexahydrotriphenylene (Cu-THQ-HHTP), for \(\mathrm{NH}_{3}\) gas-sensing applications.28,29 Despite the fastest response speed of CuTHQ-HHTP, the experimental results demonstrated that there was no noticeable synergistic \(\mathrm{NH}_{3}\) sensing performance exceeding unmixed MOFs.
One way to explore the synergy in gas adsorption is to use an additional dimension of stacking changes in the multicomponent 2D-cMOFs. By combining 2D-cMOFs with contrasting stacking tendencies, we can potentially have stacking structures that have the synergy effect due to changes in the chemical environments of the gas adsorption sites where the charge transfer mainly occurs. Cao et al. reported improved water diffusion performance with stacking changes.30 Their MD simulation results showed that water in 2D-cMOFs with eclipsed stacking resulted in a \(21.5\%\) higher diffusion coefficient than in 2D-cMOFs with slipped-parallel stacking due to increased hydrogen bonds from the zigzag geometry.30 Therefore, this approach allows us to tailor the properties of the 2D-cMOFs' gas adsorption sites to optimize their gas− MOF interaction for the sensing application. And in this paper, we present a candidate material for a mixed ligand 2D-cMOF that demonstrates this concept.
Here, we performed a density functional theory (DFT) analysis to study the tunability of stacking modes in the mixed ligand 2D-cMOF and its resulting gas adsorption capability. The mixed ligand candidate selection was based on providing geometrical diversity (different stacking modes) while maintaining chemical composition. To maintain a balance between theoretical assumption and feasibility, we considered the following three conditions: (1) the candidates should be experimentally reported, (2) consist of the same functional group (to minimize variables as we explore undiscovered materials), and (3) have the contrasting stackings. Out of 36 2D-cMOF candidates consisting of transition metals of Co, Cu, and Ni and organic ligands of HXB, HXTP, and HXTT (X = H (−OH−), I (−NH2−), and T (−SH−)),31 cobalt-
hexaiminobenzene (Co-HIB) and cobalt-2,3,6,7,10,11-hexaiminotriphenylene (Co-HITP) were selected as the only pair satisfying the aforementioned conditions. Co-HIB and Co-HITP were reported to have superior electrochemical properties\(^{32,33}\) while having contrasting stacking models, the eclipsed\(^{32}\) and slipped-parallel stackings,\(^{37}\) respectively (Figure 1). The potential energy surface (PES) calculation predicted a stacking model of Co-HIB-HITP as the slipped-parallel stacking with a much larger interlayer displacement than Co-HITP. This indicates the stacking model can be further designed and controlled by introducing a secondary ligand to the 2D-cMOF system. In addition, the adsorption properties of the mixed ligand candidate were explored by targeting toxic industrial gases, hydrogen sulfide \(\left(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{S}\right)\), ammonia \(\left(\mathrm{NH}_{3}\right)\), and nitrous oxides (NO and \(\mathrm{NO}_{2}\)). The DFT results showed that a mixed ligand system improved the gas−MOF interaction of the 2D-cMOFs, leading to a significant enhancement in the adsorption behavior toward \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{S}\) and \(\mathrm{NH}_{3}\). The adsorption energy of the mixed ligand 2D-cMOF was improved by 158 and \(170\%\) on \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{S}\) and \(\mathrm{NH}_{3}\), respectively, compared to its unmixed states, Co-HIB and Co-HITP. The electron charge density analysis results further supported the superiority of the mixed ligand architecture by showing charge transfer amounts greater by several orders of magnitude than the unmixed MOFs. We showed how the mixed ligand 2D-cMOF can result in a unique structure that exhibits improved adsorption properties compared to the single-component MOFs. This in-depth analysis of Co-HIB-HITP would provide design insight for future gas adsorption applications.
## COMPUTATIONAL METHODS
DFT Settings. All calculations were employed with the DFT method using the Vienna Ab initio Simulation Package (VASP 5.4.1).\(^{34,35}\) The DFT-D3 method with zero damping by Grimme et al. was used for van der Waals corrections.\(^{36}\) A conjugate gradient algorithm was adopted to update ionic movements. For exchange-correlation functionals, generalized gradient approximation was used with Perdew−Burke−Ernzerhof, A \(k\)-point mesh of \(2 \times 2 \times 3\) (Co
HIB and Co-HIB-HITP) and \(1 \times 1 \times 3\) (Co-HITP) was used for the Brillouin zone sampling along reciprocal lattice vectors with a \(\Gamma\)-centered scheme. The different \(k\)-point values were applied to consider the difference in lattice parameters. The cell optimization step was preceded by allowing all degrees of freedom and stress tensors for the 2D-cMOF structures, considering atomic differences among the candidates studied in this paper. The convergence was set at \(0.05\,\mathrm{eV}\) for the total ionic force and \(10^{-5}\,\mathrm{eV}\) for the electronic energy. The detailed lattice information of all 2D-cMOF structures is provided in Section 2.2 and Table S1. For electronic structure calculations, magnetic ordering and DOS, a spin-polarized DFT + U method was applied with an increased \(k\)-point mesh of \(4 \times 4 \times 6\). A U parameter of 3.3 by Wang et al. was adopted to consider correlation energy from the electron−electron interactions.\(^{37}\)
Structure Preparation and PES Construction. One distinctive feature of 2D-cMOFs, among many others including abundant active sites, pore structure, and topology,\(^{38}\) is that they can possess various stacking modes, adding an additional degree of variability of their structures. The stacking model in 2D layers aligned parallel along the \(z\)-direction is called the eclipsed stacking (i.e., AA stacking), and it has been reported in the HIB ligand\(^{32,39}\) and 2.3,6,7,10,11-triphenylene-bexathiol ligand-based 2D-cMOFs,\(^{40,41}\) coordinated with transition metals (Co, Cu, and Ni). On the other hand, the slipped-parallel stacking (i.e., AB stacking) is when the secondary layers are slipped horizontally with repeating patterns. Chen et al. successfully reported slipped interlayer displacements of three HITP-based 2D-cMOFs using powder X-ray diffraction (PXRD) and synchrotron PXRD data analysis.\(^{27}\) This structural diversity indicates that a successful 2D-cMOF-based sensing material design requires an in-depth understanding of the change in the gas−MOF interaction at different stacking models. In this sense, combining Co-HIB and Co-HITP provides a unique intersection between the two stacking models since they were reported to form AA and AB stackings, respectively.\(^{27,32}\)
The unit cell of the mixed ligand 2D-cMOF, Co-HIB-HITP, was simulated in a multilayer structure to investigate the structural impact of introducing the guest ligand with contrasting stacking tendencies. The structure of Co-HIB-HITP was assumed that the secondary ligand (e.g., HITP ligand in Co-HIB) occupied the same position based on Co-metal, and this layer repeated along the \(z\)-direction (Figure 2). The lattice parameters of the optimized AA stacking structure were calculated as \(a = b = 17.66\,\mathrm{\AA}, c = 6.76\,\mathrm{\AA}, \alpha = \beta = 90^{\circ}\),
and \(\gamma = 120^{\circ}\). To access the stacking model by plotting the PES calculations in a contour map, the interlayer displacement of the relaxed AA structure was displaced by \(0.2\,\mathrm{\AA}\) from zero to \(4.2\,\mathrm{\AA}\) along the \(ab\)-plane (Figure 2b). Periodic self-consistent field (SCF) calculations were performed at each displaced structure. As a result, 484 SCF calculations were plotted as the PES map on a 2D scale (Figure 3).
Adsorption Energy and Charge Density Difference Calculation. The adsorption energy \(\left(E_{\mathrm{ad}}\right)\) is an important indicator to access the gas−MOF interaction, which can relate to the sensing response in the experimental aspect. The adsorption energy between the 2D-cMOFs and the target gases \(\left(\mathrm{NH}_{3},\,\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{S},\,\mathrm{NO}_{\mathrm{}}\right.\) and \(\left.\mathrm{NO}_{2}\right)\) was calculated by following the equation \(E_{\mathrm{ad}}=I_{\mathrm{total}}-\left(E_{\mathrm{MOF}}+\right.\) \(E_{\mathrm{gas}})\) ), where \(E_{\mathrm{total}}\) represents the total energy of the MOF with adsorbed gas molecules, \(E_{\mathrm{MOF}}\) represents the energy of the MOF without the gas molecules, and \(E_{\mathrm{gas}}\) is the energy of the gas. The volume of the unit cell was fixed to ensure that the energy difference upon the gas introduction is purely driven by the gas adsorption process. Note that all adsorption energy calculations resulted in negative values expressed in absolute values. Therefore, the higher adsorption energy indicates stronger binding in our system. Bader charge and charge density difference (CDD) were further calculated to analyze the charge transfer behavior upon the adsorption. A negative Bader charge represents the adsorption that takes place by the gas donating electrons to the MOF. The CDD plot displays the isosurface of the electron density upon the interaction of gas molecules and the MOFs. It allows visual confirmation where the charge transfer occurs by showing the electron density accumulation and depletion. Therefore, the CDD upon the gas adsorption on the MOFs, \(\Delta\rho_{\mathrm{i}}\) , was obtained by following the equation \(\Delta\rho\,=\,\rho_{\mathrm{total}}\,-\) \(\left(\rho_{\mathrm{MOF}}+\rho_{\mathrm{gas}}\right)\) , where \(\rho_{\mathrm{total}}\) represents the electronic charge density of the MOF with adsorbed gas molecules, \(\rho_{\mathrm{MOF}}\) represents the electronic charge density of the MOF without the gas molecules, and \(\rho_{\mathrm{gas}}\) is the electronic charge density of the gas. Note that the multilayer unit cell and the same calculation methods were applied to the unmixed MOFs, Co-HIB and Co-HITP, for the comparative analysis.
## RESULTS AND DISCUSSION
Structural Analysis. Geometry and Stacking Model. The 2D-cMOF structures discussed in this study are based on an amine functional group \(\left(\mathrm{NH}_{3}\right)\), creating additional hydrogen
(H) bonds around the pore sites, unlike 2D-cMOFs based on hydroxy (OH) or thiol functional (SH) groups. The metal centers and aromatic ligand frames are surrounded by H atoms, making the MOF pore sites an H-rich environment (Figure 1). Therefore, accessing the stacking model of 2D-cMOFs is important because the variation in the interlayer distance may further vary the atomic environment of the gas adsorption sites. The AA stacking enhances the H-rich environment as possessing the repeating \(-\mathrm{NH}\) bonds along the \(c\)-axis. The AB stacking, however, induces non-H sides in the MOF to be exposed to the pore sites as the secondary layers migrate along the \(ab\)-axis (Figure 2b).
The stacking models of the unmixed 2D-cMOFs were predicted with the PES analysis by Jeon et al., and the results agreed well with the experimental reports. A global minimum potential energy of Co-HIB was reported as \(-551.46\,\mathrm{eV}\) at AA stacking, which corresponds to \((0,\ 0)\) Å in the PES plot (Figure S1a). A local minimum energy area was also observed at AB stacking coordinates of \((2.0,\,4.2)\) and \((4.2,\ 2.0)\) Å as \(-551.38\,\mathrm{eV}\), representing the possibility of stable stacking at the AB stacking model. Still, the lowest potential energy at the AA region indicates a much favorable stacking stability of Co-HIB in the eclipsed model over the slipped-parallel model. The PES of Co-HITP demonstrated strong AB tendency, resulting in four distinctive global minimum energy regions with an equal energy value of \(>1074.99\,\mathrm{eV}\) at \((0.8,\,4.0)\), \((3.2,\,4.0)\), \((4.0,\,0.8)\), and \((4.0,\,3.2)\) Å (Figure S1b). Furthermore, the global minimum energy region showed broad distribution from 1.4 to \(4.2\,\mathrm{\AA}\) along the \(ab\)-plane. This indicates that the Co-HITP may possess a stable stacking structure for the interlayer displacements corresponding to \(1.4{-}4.2\,\mathrm{\AA}\) regions along the \(ab\)-plane. In fact, Chen et al. reported the interlayer displacement of Co-HITP as \((0,\ 1.39)\) Å based on Pawley refinements of synchrotron PXRD patterns, which was consistent with the PES prediction.
Interestingly, the PES of Co-HIB-HITP showed a further shifting tendency toward AB displacement compared to its unmixed states (Figure 3). The global minimum energy was observed around four coordinates of \((1.2,\,4.2)\), \((4.2,\,1.2)\), \((3.0,\,4.2)\), and \((4.2,\ 3.0)\) Å. Their potential energy values were almost identical, \(-813.23\,\mathrm{eV}\) at \((1.2,\,4.2)\) Å and \(-813.22\,\mathrm{eV}\) at the remaining displacements. The lowest energy distribution of the mixed ligand 2D-cMOF became much narrower to \(3.6{-}4.2\) Å along the \(ab\)-plane compared to the Co-HITP. This indicates that the Co-HIB-HITP may possess stable stacking at larger interlayer displacements around \(3.6{-}4.2\,\mathrm{\AA}\) regions along the \(ab\)-plane compared to the unmixed MOFs. Furthermore, this shifting tendency shows that the interlayer stacking may be designed and controlled by introducing guest ligands. The increment in the interlayer displacement suggests possibility in the enhanced gas−MOF interaction due to the change in the atomic environment in the pore sites, and it was discussed in Section 3.2.
large variation in potential binding sites (i.e., pore diameters \(= 9.32 \, \text{\AA}\) for Co-HIB, \(13.17 \, \text{\AA}\) for Co-HIB-HITP, and \(17.64 \, \text{\AA}\) for Co-HITP) relative to the small size of the guest molecules with an average of \(1.2 \, \text{\AA}\) bond length. Previous studies have revealed that the chemiresistive response is derived from the competitive interchange between the redox activity of metal centers\(^{19,21,49}\) and surface acidity assisting from the organic ligands in \(2 \, \text{D-cMOFs}^{50-62}\) when the gas−MOF interaction occurs. Still, the discussions remained on Cu- and Ni-based 2D-MOFs, leaving the Co-based mixed ligand system unexplored. Therefore, possible binding sites were all accessed to identify unique adsorption behaviors with the target guest molecules (Figure S2). The gas orientations and binding sites that yielded the highest adsorption energy were considered the most favorable. Finally, our PES analysis showed that more than one stacking coordinate was predicted for each MOF system to be stably synthesized. This stacking diversity of the 2D-cMOFs may induce different gas−MOF interactions as the secondary layers are exposed to different sections of the gas molecules, moving along the planar coordinates.\(^{30}\) Therefore, the adsorption energy calculations were analyzed on multiple global and local minimum PES coordinates, considering the variation of the gas orientation and the binding sites to reveal the impact of the stacking displacement in each MOF system (Table S4).\(^{30}\)
The adsorption energies were analyzed based on the PES-predicted stacking models (i.e., the global minimum PES coordinates), which correspond to the AA stacking for Co-HIB and the AB stacking models for Co-HITP and Co-HIB-HITP. When discussing the adsorption energy of the AB stacking models, the symmetrical displacement coordinates, such as (0.8, 4.0) and (4.0, 0.8) Å, were considered identical in the case of unmixed MOFs, Co-HIB and Co-HITP. In contrast, the symmetrical coordinates of the mixed ligand MOF were studied individually due to the structural asymmetry. It is because the Co-metal of the mixed ligand MOF is connected with different organic ligands and this asymmetry results in contrasting gas−MOF interactions between the coordinates (Figure S3). The effect of stacking change due to the gas−MOF interaction was assumed to be negligible, considering its small change (Table S5). The displacement with the highest adsorption energy was considered the most favorable.
As a result, the overall adsorption energy trend showed a unique synergy effect of Co-HIB-HITP toward \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{S}\) and \(\mathrm{NH}_{3}\) (Figure 4). The \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{S}\) adsorption energies of Co-HIB and Co
HITP were calculated as 28.65 and \(38.47 \, \text{kJ/mol}\), respectively. Co-HIB-HITP, however, resulted in \(60.88 \, \text{kJ/mol}\), showing a \(158\%\) improvement in the adsorption energy compared to the largest value of the unmixed MOFs. The gas−MOF distance was measured as \(2.59 \, \text{\AA}\) when \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{S}\) gas was exposed to the Co-HIB-HITP, which was significantly reduced compared to 3.2 and \(3.15 \, \text{\AA}\) in the case of Co-HIB and Co-HITP (Figure S4a−c). The synergistic adsorption behavior was further enhanced in \(\mathrm{NH}_{3}\) gas by \(170\%\) with \(64.78 \, \text{kJ/mol}\), while Co-HIB and Co-HITP resulted in 18.44 and \(38.06 \, \text{kJ/mol}\), respectively. The gas−MOF distance in Co-HIB-HITP was \(1.99 \, \text{\AA}\) near the Co-metal site, while Co-HIB and Co-HITP recorded 3.2 and 3.18 Å (Figure S4a−f). The results suggested a strong physisorption induced by the mixed ligand system toward \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{S}\) and \(\mathrm{NH}_{3}\).
On the other hand, the NO and \(\mathrm{NO}_{2}\) gases showed interpolated adsorption energy trends contrary to the above two cases. The NO adsorption energy of the mixed ligand 2D-cMOF was only \(46.41 \, \text{kJ/mol}\), which resulted in between \(85.81 \, \text{kJ/mol}\) for Co-HIB and \(35.1 \, \text{kJ/mol}\) for Co-HITP. The corresponding gas−MOF binding distance was 2.226, 2.35, and \(2.4 \, \text{\AA}\) (Figure S4g−i). The \(\mathrm{NO}_{2}\) showed a similar interpolated result that Co-HIB-HITP recorded \(128.74 \, \text{kJ/mol}\) and Co-HIB and Co-HITP were 222.27 and \(72.12 \, \text{kJ/mol}\), respectively. The corresponding binding distances suggested competitive and strong gas−MOF interactions as 1.85, 1.88, and \(1.99 \, \text{\AA}\) (Figure S4j−l). The large adsorption energies above \(80 \, \text{kJ/mol}\) are understood as the strong oxidizing nature of NO and \(\mathrm{NO}_{2}\) gases and have been widely reported for the conductive 2D materials.\(^{42,44,48,53-56}\) Consideration of surface interactions by simulating the monolayer system also resulted in the interpolated trend toward NO and \(\mathrm{NO}_{2}\) gases (Figure S7). To summarize, the adsorption energy trend showed a significant synergy effect in the \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{S}\) and \(\mathrm{NH}_{3}\) gas adsorption energy of Co-HIB-HITP, while NO and \(\mathrm{NO}_{2}\) resulted in interpolated adsorption energy values between Co-HIB and Co-HITP. However, the results still leave a question on the root cause of the gas-selective synergistic adsorption in the mixed ligand 2D-cMOF.
Additional adsorption energy calculation was further designed to clarify the unique synergy effect on \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{S}\) and \(\mathrm{NH}_{3}\) by investigating the impact of the stacking models. The adsorption energies at AA and AB stacking modes were calculated in addition to the PES-predicted coordinates, and the result showed that energetically favorable stacking differs by the target gas types. The hydrogen-containing gases, \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{S}\) and \(\mathrm{NH}_{3}\), exhibited strong AB stacking preference regardless of MOF types (Figure 5 and Table S4). The \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{S}\) adsorption energies at AB stacking were enhanced by an average of \(232\%\) from the energies at the AA stacking. The \(\mathrm{NH}_{3}\) further broadened the gap by showing \(371\%\) greater adsorption energies over the AA model. Interestingly, the energetically favorable stacking was reversed to the AA model in the cases of non-hydrogen-containing gases, NO and \(\mathrm{NO}_{2}\) (Figure 5). The average reduction of AB over AA stackings was \(74\%\) in the case of NO gas. The \(\mathrm{NO}_{2}\) adsorption energies all exceeded \(70 \, \text{kJ/mol}\) in the AB models, showing an average reduction of \(69\%\) over the AA model. The shift in the favorable stacking model explains the gas-selective synergy effect in Co-HIB-HITP shown in Figure 4. Co-HIB-HITP was predicted to have the largest interlayer displacement compared to the unmixed MOFs. Consequentially, this leads us to synergistically strong adsorption toward \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{S}\) and \(\mathrm{NH}_{3}\) gases because these
hydrogen-containing gases energetically favor the AB stacking (whereas it is the opposite for NO and \(\mathrm{NO}_{2}\)).
Gas Adsorption Mechanism Analysis. Charge Transfer and Gas−MOF Interaction. Understanding the 2D-cMOF geometries based on the \(-\mathrm{NH}_{2}\) functional groups further
clarifies the superiority of the mixed ligand MOF over the unmixed MOFs. As mentioned in Section 3.1, the pore sites of the MOF candidates create the H-rich environment owing to the unpaired valence orbitals of nitrogen atoms in the MOFs. The stacking model and the accessible binding site become a
driving force of effective gas−MOF adsorption, promoting charge transfer. The Bader charge and the CDD analysis successfully revealed these trends.
The most significant difference between Co-HIB and Co-HIB-HITP is their contrasting stacking tendency; Co-HIB was predicted as AA stacking, while Co-HIB-HITP resulted in AB stacking. The electron charge density analysis suggested that the slipped-parallel layers of Co-HIB-HITP are advantageous compared to the eclipsed layers of Co-HIB in the case of \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{S}\) and \(\mathrm{NH}_{3}\) adsorption. When these H-containing gases were exposed to the 2D-cMOFs, the secondary layers with the slipped-parallel structure allowed an additional charge transfer as \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{S}\) atoms of the gases directly interact with the electronegative regions of the 2D layers along the \(z\)-direction. The CDD isosurface showed that large charge density clouds were created between the \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{S}\) and \(\mathrm{NH}_{3}\) gases and the slipped-parallel layers upon adsorption (Figure 6c and Figure S5c). The eclipsed layers of Co-HIB, however, are disadvantageous in terms of the electrostatic interaction since the H atoms located in the upper and lower layers cause the same charge repulsion with H atoms in the guest molecules. As a result, the AA stacking of Co-HIB hinders the effective charge transfer induced by the interaction with the secondary layers. The charge density clouds around the H-containing gases and the eclipsed layers significantly reduced in the Co-HIB (Figure 6a and Figure S5a). The charge transfer amount of the gas on Co-HIB-HITP at the AB stacking was greater by an average of \(345\%\) than on Co-HIB at the AA stacking, representing their dominance on the H-containing gas adsorption (Table S5 and Figure S6).
On the other hand, the non-H-containing gases, NO and \(\mathrm{NO}_{2}\), showed that effective electrostatic interactions were much accelerated in the AA stacking model owing to the direct contact with H-rich regions of the host material. The strong oxidizing nature of NO and \(\mathrm{NO}_{2}\) favors the eclipsed layers to have direct \(\mathrm{N}-\mathrm{H}\) and \(\mathrm{O}-\mathrm{H}\) attractions, inducing the unique synergy effect toward the H-containing gases of the mixed ligand MOF. The CDD isosurface showed that the charge density clouds along the \(z\)-direction were further developed in the AA model (Figure 6d and Figure S5d). The slipped-parallel layers, however, resulted in the same charge repulsion between the gas and the secondary layers of the host material, leading to a decrease in charge transfer from the secondary layers (Figure 6f and Figure S5f). The superior charge transfer of AA over AB further supports their dominance on the non-H-containing gas adsorption (Table S5). Since the strong adsorption toward all target gases results in a lack of selectivity and failure of gas desorption, Co-HIB-HITP's gas-selective synergistic effect can be advantageous for the H-containing gas sensing.
Understanding the change in the energetically favorable stacking model by the target gases simplifies the analysis of the superiority of Co-HIB-HITP over Co-HITP; Co-HITP was also predicted as AB stacking while exhibiting poorer adsorption behavior than the mixed ligand 2D-cMOF. This is mainly attributed to the difference in metal sites with which the target gases can interact. The combination of HIB and HITP ligands generated a unique curvature around the target gas molecules with a medium pore diameter of \(13.17\,\mathrm{\AA}\) (Figure 1). As the short length of the HIB ligand reduced the distance between cobalt metals by \(4.26\,\mathrm{\AA}\), Co-HIB-HITP has a metal density of \(5.25\%\) (percent ratio of cobalt atoms in the unit cell), while the metal density of Co-HITP has only appeared as \(4\%\). The higher metal density improves the electrostatic
interaction between the gas and the MOF as the uncoordinated Co-metal center interacts more strongly with gas molecules. The charge density clouds were developed based on the HIB-ligand side where the metal density is much higher than the HITP-ligand site (Figure 6 and Figure S8), resulting in enhanced charge transfer than Co-HITP regardless of the adsorbed gas molecules (Table S6). Its improved accessibility toward Co-metal sites can also explain the competitive adsorption properties of Co-HIB-HITP and Co-HIB in AB stacking, as Co-HIB has a metal density of \(7.69\%\) (Figure 5). The change in the magnetic moments further clarifies the effect of metal density. Our calculation showed that the changes in the magnetic moments upon the slow adsorption dominantly occurred toward Co-HIB and Co-HIB-HITP (Table S7). The average alterations in the total magnetic moments at PES-predicted stacking were \(0.484\,\mu\mathrm{B}\) for Co-HIB and \(1.642\,\mu\mathrm{B}\) for Co-HIB-HITP. Co-HITP, on the other hand, exhibited an average magnetic moment change of \(0.249\,\mu\mathrm{B}\), about half the change compared to 2D-cMOFs with the HIB ligand. As the magnetic moment is predominantly influenced by the presence of unpaired electrons of molecular orbitals, this significant change indicated that a strong gas−MOF interaction occurred at the unpaired electrons in cobalt's 3d orbitals. Despite the superior metal density of Co-HIB, the mixed ligand MOF can possess a much promising position from a practical point of view since a narrow pore channel size below \(10\,\mathrm{\AA}\) of Co-HIB may lead to poor sensor recovery. Furthermore, the analysis of spin-polarized DOS showed that Co-HIB-HITP possesses the highest peak, also notably sharper, indicating a larger density of available electronic states at the energy of the peak (Figure S10). This observation suggests enhanced metallic characteristics, potentially advantageous in the gas−MOF interaction for the H-containing gases.
## CONCLUSIONS
In this study, the mixed ligand 2D-cMOF, Co-HIB-HITP, was investigated as the novel gas-sensing material based on the DFT method. The PES analysis predicted the structural stability of Co-HIB-HITP at larger interlayer displacements around \(3.6-4.2\,\mathrm{\AA}\) regions along the \(ab\)-plane than its unmixed states, Co-HIB and Co-HITP. This indicates the tunability of the stacking model by introducing the guest ligand. The variation in the stacking led to the variation in the gas adsorption behavior as the host material induced different gas−MOF interactions. The gas adsorption capabilities were analyzed by the adsorption energy calculation on small toxic gases, \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{S}\), \(\mathrm{NH}_{3}\), \(\mathrm{NO}\), and \(\mathrm{NO}_{2}\). The comparative study revealed that the \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{S}\) and \(\mathrm{NH}_{3}\) adsorption energies were improved by 158 and \(170\%\) in Co-HIB-HITP compared to the unmixed MOFs. Further analysis revealed that this unique gas-selective synergy effect was caused by the energetically favorable stacking models that vary with the characteristics of the target gases. The electron charge density analysis suggested that \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{S}\) and \(\mathrm{NH}_{3}\) favor AB stacking, while \(\mathrm{NO}\) and \(\mathrm{NO}_{2}\) favor AA stacking due to the enhanced electrostatic interactions.
The synergy effect in \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{S}\) and \(\mathrm{NH}_{3}\) gas adsorptions was attributed to the highest interlayer displacement and an intermediate metal density of Co-HIB-HITP. In the case of Co-HIB and Co-HITP, Co-HIB was predicted to have a high Co-metal density of \(7.69\%\) but an AA stacking mode, while Co-HITP showed a low Co-metal density of \(4\%\) but an AB stacking mode. The adsorption energy and the electron charge density calculations revealed that Co-HIB-HITP's AB stacked
layers improved the gas−MOF interaction for the hydrogen-containing gases compared to Co-HIB of AA stacking, despite having an intermediate metal density of \(5.25\%\). It is because the amine functional groups and Co-metal were exposed to the binding sites by the slipped-parallel layers, enhancing the electrostatic interaction with \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{S}\) and \(\mathrm{NH}_{3}\). Although Co-HITP and Co-HIB-HITP were both analyzed as AB stacking structures, the \(1.25\%\) higher metal density of the mixed ligand 2D-cMOF resulted in superior gas adsorption energy and charge transfer behavior regardless of the adsorbed molecules. Consequentially, the gas-selective strong adsorption and enhanced charge transfer confirmed the superiority of Co-HIB-HITP owing to its unique structural advantages. To gain a comprehensive understanding of the gas adsorption mechanism in the mixed ligand 2D-cMOF, it is essential to investigate the observed variations in adsorption energy trends. Therefore, further analysis of the electronic structure, including the DOS and magnetic moments, may be necessary to provide in-depth insights into the nature of gas adsorption and the origins of the observed synergy effects. This approach may clarify the role of each component of the 2D-cMOFs, such as metal and functional groups in the mixed system, which will help us to better understand the multicomponent MOFs in future research. Furthermore, it is important to explore experimental synthesis techniques and conduct sensing performance tests, such as evaluating response and recovery times and assessing durability. However, the first DFT mechanism study of the mixed ligand 2D-cMOF system inspires the material design perspective for the next-generation MOF-based gas-sensing application.
